In conventional process design, nominal values of  X ,  V ,  W , and  Z  are determined by simulations, empirical knowledge, or standards [ 8 ]. During production, however, deviations in material behavior, lubrication, tool geometry, machine stiffness, or process control may lead to deviations in the target geometry. A process can therefore be considered robust if the resulting geometrical property remains within a defined tolerance window: 2 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
